-- title:
--   Completing a point of order N to a basis of A[N]
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $B$ be additive commutative groups and let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$. Assume given an isomorphism of additive groups $e \colon \mathbb{Z}/N \times \mathbb{Z}/N \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; \{P \in A : N \cdot P = 0\}$, the target being the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $A$ annihilated by $N$ (`Submodule.torsionBy ℤ A N`). Let $Q \in A$ have additive order exactly $N$, and let $\varphi \colon A \to B$ be a homomorphism of additive groups whose kernel is precisely the subgroup $\mathbb{Z}Q$ of integer multiples of $Q$. Then there exists $R \in A$ such that: $N \cdot R = 0$; the additive order of $\varphi(R)$ is exactly $N$; and for every $P \in A$ with $N \cdot P = 0$ one has $\varphi(P) \in \mathbb{Z}\varphi(R)$. Thus the image under $\varphi$ of the $N$-torsion of $A$ is contained in the cyclic group generated by $\varphi(R)$, and that group has order $N$; the conclusion is stated as an inclusion rather than an equality, although $R$ itself is $N$-torsion.
--
--   This is the group-theoretic content of the statement that, for an isogeny of degree $N$ with cyclic kernel of order $N$ between elliptic curves, the image of the full $N$-torsion is cyclic of order $N$: a point of order $N$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/N)^2$ can be completed to a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis, the second basis vector providing the required generator. It is used in the computation of ramification indices and stabiliser orders for maps between modular curves, in [`ModularCurve.ord_sub_mul_natCard_stabilizer_zmultiples_reduceHom_eq_ramificationIndexAlong_mul_natCard_stabilizer_fullKernelQuotient`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ord_sub_mul_natCard_stabilizer_zmultiples_reduceHom_eq_ramificationIndexAlong_mul_natCard_stabilizer_fullKernelQuotient).
